  "answer": "### Short Answer\n\n### Interpretation\nTo compute the net taxable estate, the gross estate, which includes all properties owned by the decedent at the time of death, is reduced by allowable deductions such as funeral expenses, debts, and other expenses related to the estate. The estate tax is then applied to the net taxable estate at rates that increase progressively, starting from 0% for estates valued at P0 to P200,000, and reaching up to 6% for estates exceeding P10 million.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that for estates valued below P200,000, no estate tax is due. For estates exceeding this threshold, the tax liability increases with the value of the estate, which can significantly impact the heirs' inheritance. Proper planning and valuation of the estate are crucial to ensure compliance and optimize tax liabilities.",
